1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,549
Welcome, everybody, to the lovely house and gardens of Rosewood, once the home of

2
00:00:06,549 --> 00:00:09,000
the famous writer, Sebastian George.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,456
He bought the house in 1902 although he had first seen it two years

4
00:00:14,456 --> 00:00:15,000
earlier.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,038
At that time the owners let it out to a tenant

6
00:00:18,038 --> 00:00:21,620
because George was too slow making up his mind to buy it.

7
00:00:21,620 --> 00:00:25,090
When it came back on the market, there was no hesitation

8
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:32,751
and he bought it immediately, for ￡9,300, even though the house had no bathroom,

9
00:00:32,751 --> 00:00:34,717
no running water upstairs,

10
00:00:34,717 --> 00:00:35,940
and no electricity.

11
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:40,320
When he came here, he'd been married for ten years.

12
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:45,347
During that time, he'd become one of the most famous writers in the English-speaking

13
00:00:45,347 --> 00:00:45,810
world.

14
00:00:45,810 --> 00:00:48,508
His professional success was enormous,

15
00:00:48,508 --> 00:00:51,910
but his personal life wasn't as successful.

16
00:00:51,910 --> 00:00:55,063
He was no longer on speaking terms with his brother

17
00:00:55,063 --> 00:00:59,823
and had been devastated by the death at the age of seven of his

18
00:00:59,823 --> 00:01:01,630
elder daughter, Josephine.

19
00:01:01,630 --> 00:01:06,590
Moving to Rosewood allowed the family to start a new life.

20
00:01:06,590 --> 00:01:12,676
George regarded Rosewood as a pure example of a traditional country house of this part of England

21
00:01:12,676 --> 00:01:15,870
and did some of his most successful writing here.

22
00:01:15,870 --> 00:01:22,817
The house and its grounds became the family haven and their escape to privacy

23
00:01:22,817 --> 00:01:23,500
and quiet.

24
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:24,803
The walls,

25
00:01:24,803 --> 00:01:30,257
and the mullioned windows were built of the local sandstone, the tiles on the roofs

26
00:01:30,257 --> 00:01:34,347
and the bricks of the chimney stack were baked from local clay,

27
00:01:34,347 --> 00:01:39,600
and the wooden structures inside came from oak trees which grow around here.

28
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:45,080
Now, please look at the map I've given you of the house and gardens.

29
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:48,650
We're here at the Information Centre.

30
00:01:48,650 --> 00:01:54,191
Follow the path marked with the arrow and the first area you come to

31
00:01:54,191 --> 00:01:56,500
is the orchard on your left.

32
00:01:56,500 --> 00:02:01,567
As you go further down the path, there's the kitchen garden on the right

33
00:02:01,567 --> 00:02:05,893
and as you go round the first sharp corner you will find, to your

34
00:02:05,893 --> 00:02:11,101
left, an area where different types of pear tree have been planted as well

35
00:02:11,101 --> 00:02:12,403
as some lovely flowers,

36
00:02:12,403 --> 00:02:17,550
and this is known as Pear Alley-designed by George himself.

37
00:02:17,550 --> 00:02:24,290
Next to this is the greenhouse where some exotic plants and fruits are grown.

38
00:02:24,290 --> 00:02:29,045
Follow the path round the second corner and on your right you will see

39
00:02:29,045 --> 00:02:34,080
the entrance to the Mulberry Garden with its 500-year-old tree.

40
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:39,707
Past the Mulberry Garden, follow the path until you reach the front of the

41
00:02:39,707 --> 00:02:40,310
house.

42
00:02:40,310 --> 00:02:44,846
I suggest you spend a good hour wandering around this lovely building.

43
00:02:44,846 --> 00:02:48,820
A guide takes visitor groups round every two hours.

44
00:02:48,820 --> 00:02:52,591
If you would like to purchase any of George's books

45
00:02:52,591 --> 00:02:57,871
or other souvenirs, then leave the house by the side entrance where you will

46
00:02:57,871 --> 00:02:59,003
find our shop,

47
00:02:59,003 --> 00:03:04,283
which is situated between the house and the garage which contains the magnificent old

48
00:03:04,283 --> 00:03:07,300
Rolls-Royce car which used to belong to George.

49
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:12,348
I expect by this time you may also be in need of a rest

50
00:03:12,348 --> 00:03:13,430
and some refreshment.

51
00:03:13,430 --> 00:03:14,505
Most visitors are,

52
00:03:14,505 --> 00:03:19,522
so why don't you visit the tea room on the far side of the

53
00:03:19,522 --> 00:03:19,880
garage?

54
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:26,090
If you have time, there is a lovely walk down towards the River Dudwell.

55
00:03:26,090 --> 00:03:29,430
For me, this is the best part of the estate.

56
00:03:29,430 --> 00:03:34,290
This isn't on the map but it is all clearly signposted.

57
00:03:34,290 --> 00:03:38,310
You cross the field which spreads along the banks of the river.

58
00:03:38,310 --> 00:03:41,890
In spring, this area is well worth a visit.

59
00:03:41,890 --> 00:03:46,822
Spend a minute or two watching the water pass by underneath as you cross

60
00:03:46,822 --> 00:03:47,527
the footbridge,

61
00:03:47,527 --> 00:03:51,050
and then continue along the River Walk through the woodland.

62
00:03:51,050 --> 00:03:56,320
On a hot summer's day the trees along this path provide welcome shade.

63
00:03:56,320 --> 00:04:02,005
Eventually you come to the water mill which used to provide the electricity for

64
00:04:02,005 --> 00:04:05,660
the house-only about four hours every evening-in George's time.

65
00:04:05,660 --> 00:04:11,432
And, finally, for those of you who would like to see stunning views of the surrounding countryside

66
00:04:11,432 --> 00:04:14,149
and who are a little bit more energetic,

67
00:04:14,149 --> 00:04:18,563
when you return from the mill take the first turning on your left

68
00:04:18,563 --> 00:04:20,600
and climb up to the viewpoint.

69
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:22,000
You won't regret it.

70
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:23,790
Enjoy your visit!
